{"id":"o-troutdalehistory-org","claimKey":"83d3973c772985c12055c43bba5e20431728dd0888ccb96693677a1aa09fc692","title":"Depot Rail Museum","cat":"play","art":"museum","area":"Troutdale, OR","metroId":"portland","src":"troutdalehistory.org","specs":[],"options":[{"name":"Memberships","detail":"Gift Membership","price":15},{"name":"Memberships","detail":"Individual Membership","price":20},{"name":"Memberships","detail":"Family Membership","price":30},{"name":"Memberships","detail":"Lovers Oak Membership","price":100},{"name":"Barn Exhibit Hall","detail":"","price":null}],"services":[{"name":"Barn Exhibit Hall","desc":"Located in historic Troutdale, Oregon, the Barn Exhibit Hall Museum preserves the history of the Columbia River Gorge, Troutdale’s agricultural roots, and the Historic Columbia River Highway. The Barn Exhibit Hall Museum opened its first major exhibit in 1998 with Smelt Run! A Fish Story, created in celebration of the Troutdale Historical Society’s 30th anniversary. The exhibit explored the history of the Sandy River smelt runs, a seasonal tradition that once brought thousands of visitors to Troutdale each year. Through historic photographs, artifacts, and interpretive displays, guests experienced an important part of the community’s cultural and economic history. Additional exhibits soon","photo":"https://static.wixstatic.com/media/1bf0fe_17509e7568ee4d3d9972fa42acf5fd6a~mv2.webp/v1/fill/w_1600,h_1000,al_c,q_85/1bf0fe_17509e7568ee4d3d9972fa42acf5fd6a~mv2.webp","variants":[{"label":"Standard","price":null,"optionIdx":4}]}],"includes":[],"addons":[],"gap":"Prices, hours and eligibility are not copied here yet. We will ask when you request.","blurb":"On November 20, 1882 a rail line was built through what is now Troutdale and continued east through the Columbia River Gorge. Troutdale’s founder, Captain John Harlow, asked railroad builders to establish a depot at his town site.","cover":"https://static.wixstatic.com/media/1bf0fe_1ae3b2ce412e46d4b91cdfb0bdd010